Clinical Guidelines for the Management of Common NCDs, Injuries and Mental Health Conditions (NCDIMH) | Chapter 7: Nephrology > Glomerular Disease > Screening and Diagnosis of Acute Glomerulonephritis (page 225)

Acute glomerulonephritis / nephrotic syndrome: investigations and differential diagnosis (primary, secondary, genetic, systemic, inflammatory and other causes) (part 1 of 2).
- Urine dipsticks<br/>- Urinalysis with microscopy<br/>- 24-hour urine protein<br/>- Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine<br/>- Full blood count<br/>- Complement studies (C3, C4)<br/>- Serum lipids<br/>- Serum electrolytes<br/>- Confirm the presence of nephrotic syndrome (albumin, cholesterol, urine dipstick & spot urine protein/creatinine ratio)<br/>- Exclude an underlying systemic or exogenous cause of nephrotic syndrome that may require treatment (diabetes, HIV, VDRL, hepatitis B, lupus serology, ANA and anti-ds DNA antibody etc., underlying malignancy e.g. myeloma causing amyloidosis)<br/>- Determine renal function (creatinine, GFR – note may be falsely low because of low muscle mass)<br/>- Exclude complications of nephrotic syndrome (sepsis from urinary losses of immunoglobulins – e.g. ascitic tap; Doppler studies to exclude venous thrombosis)<br/>- Determine the need for a kidney biopsy (e.g. in children: deteriorating kidney function, refractory proteinuria): Differential Diagnosis: 1. Primary nephrotic syndromes: minimal change disease (MCD); focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS); membranous nephropathy; membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN)<br/>2. Secondary causes: systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE);
